WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.500
Phasing is a way to simulate a construction schedule.

2
00:00:05.150 --> 00:00:11.750
You organize phases on a timeline calendar and then hide or unhide components as needed for each phase.

3
00:00:12.570 --> 00:00:16.269
Open the file named For Phasing that's been provided to you.

4
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:20.019
Go to the Media Phasing Group panel.

5
00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:27.989
Note that there's already a media file defined. We'll look at it, although you'll be creating your own in a second.

6
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.299
Click the Phasing 1 thumbnail to open the timeline for it.

7
00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:41.020
If you want, you can click the Zoom Out tool to view the extents of the phases, 9 phases that take about a year of construction work.

8
00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:52.380
If you scrub through the timeline, you can see the work evolving, from the main excavations all the way to fully constructed and inhabited projects.

9
00:00:58.119 --> 00:01:01.869
Exit Media mode. Let's look at how we can set this up.

10
00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:09.180
First thing you need to do is to define phasing content in the scene graph and scene graph containers.

11
00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.180
Here it's already been done for you. Hide all the phase containers to effectively hide your project.

12
00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:31.090
Unhide them one at a time to see their content, and you'll have an idea how they were sorted.

13
00:01:36.549 --> 00:01:40.150
Go to the Phasing panel and create a new phasing calendar.

14
00:01:41.579 --> 00:01:50.579
With the clip selected, make sure the container named Phase 1 is displayed in the scene graph and all other phase containers hidden.

15
00:01:52.359 --> 00:01:55.459
This represents the start of the work, the excavation work.

16
00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:58.829
Click Create Phase again to add a second clip.

17
00:02:02.079 --> 00:02:10.180
Unhide Phases 2 and 3 and hide Phase 1. Excavation is done and we have the foundations in place.

18
00:02:11.460 --> 00:02:13.860
Click Create Phase again to add a third clip.

19
00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:21.780
This time, hide Phase 2, the workers and their equipment, and unhide Phase 4 as the walls start coming up.

20
00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:29.710
If you make a mistake, you can select the clip on the timeline and review what needs to be visible or hidden.

21
00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:37.620
Keep on going but make sure you select the last clip on the timeline before proceeding.

22
00:02:38.689 --> 00:02:42.789
Add other clips and adjust the visibility of containers accordingly.

23
00:02:56.830 --> 00:03:00.030
At any time, you can scrub through the timeline to check your work.

24
00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:12.490
This is easy enough, but how do you output an animation of this phasing work?

25
00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:15.780
This is done by going to the Media video panel.

26
00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:27.860
Earlier, we used the video tool to keyframe camera motion but here, we just set it up to read the phasing calendar we defined.

27
00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.000
Create a new video and set it to about 30 seconds in length as before.

28
00:03:36.289 --> 00:03:51.879
Go back one step and rename the video Mic Phasing but more importantly, expand the Details sub-panel and set the Phasing group to the new phasing definition you just created.

29
00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:01.539
That's it. From this point on, you simply need to output that video to disk as you have already learned.

30
00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.150
This marks the end of this class. I hope you have enjoyed it.
